just to get some more
insight into what's going on.

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:07.391
And then when I click
that, this is telling me

00:29:07.391 --> 00:29:09.830
what percentage of
those transactions

00:29:09.830 --> 00:29:11.750
our fraud system
picked up and denied,

00:29:11.750 --> 00:29:13.880
and then what percentage
it didn't and we actually

00:29:13.880 --> 00:29:14.690
had to charge back.

00:29:14.690 --> 00:29:17.600
That's where we're
losing the money.

00:29:17.600 --> 00:29:20.690
So keeping that in mind, I
want to investigate further

00:29:20.690 --> 00:29:23.360
by looking at the actual
points of compromise.

00:29:23.360 --> 00:29:28.400
So as I said, that's day
166, which is two weeks ago--

00:29:28.400 --> 00:29:29.820
14 days.

00:29:29.820 --> 00:29:33.080
And so the whole point of
CPP is that you can actually

00:29:33.080 --> 00:29:35.090
change the time
window that you want

00:29:35.090 --> 00:29:36.950
the algorithm to look back.

00:29:36.950 --> 00:29:38.960
Right now it's
looking back two days,

00:29:38.960 --> 00:29:43.080
so the customers that went
to merchants two days prior.

00:29:43.080 --> 00:29:46.282
And if I move that, I can
actually change the time window

00:29:46.282 --> 00:29:49.440
to 14 days.

00:29:49.440 --> 00:29:51.940
So then this gives me a
list of every merchant

00:29:51.940 --> 00:29:54.690
that we believe
could be compromised,

00:29:54.690 --> 00:29:56.800
sorted in probability.

00:29:56.800 --> 00:29:58.480
And then it also
gives me the list.

00:29:58.480 --> 00:30:00.490
Once I click that,
it gives me the list

00:30:00.490 --> 00:30:04.170
of clients or customers
sorted in the same order.

00:30:04.170 --> 00:30:07.479
So I can click the
top client, and it

00:30:07.479 --> 00:30:08.520
tells me it's John Smith.

00:30:08.520 --> 00:30:10.740
This is just a splash page
of all of the information

00:30:10.740 --> 00:30:12.095
that we have on him.

00:30:12.095 --> 00:30:13.110
You can see his balance.

00:30:13.110 --> 00:30:15.160
He's got $2,000 in his account.

00:30:15.160 --> 00:30:19.374
His credit score is 145,
so he's not doing too hot.

00:30:19.374 --> 00:30:20.790
Just a whole bunch
of information,

00:30:20.790 --> 00:30:23.297
account transaction history.

00:30:23.297 --> 00:30:24.630
But this is an important number.

00:30:24.630 --> 00:30:26.516
Here is the fraud score.

00:30:26.516 --> 00:30:28.140
That's a number that
we actually assign

00:30:28.140 --> 00:30:31.560
to him based on the
likelihood that we

00:30:31.560 --> 00:30:35.555
think his information is going
to be used illegally soon.

00:30:35.555 --> 00:30:39.020
So since I know that his
fraud score is eight,

00:30:39.020 --> 00:30:40.730
so I already want to
investigate further.

00:30:40.730 --> 00:30:43.610
I want to see what
merchants he's

00:30:43.610 --> 00:30:47.690
been to, and then the different
other customers that have also

00:30:47.690 --> 00:30:49.520
been to those merchants.

00:30:49.520 --> 00:30:52.640
So I can see these small circles
represent different account

00:30:52.640 --> 00:30:55.760
holders that we have, and then
the big circles are obviously

00:30:55.760 --> 00:30:58.590
the merchants that
they've been to.

00:30:58.590 --> 00:30:59.956
So I can see John Smith.

00:30:59.956 --> 00:31:01.080
So this is his fraud score.

00:31:01.080 --> 00:31:02.490
It's eight still.

00:31:02.490 --> 00:31:04.140
He's that little
circle there, and he's

00:31:04.140 --> 00:31:10.130
been to Best Buy, Target,
Walgreens, Al's Toy Store.

00:31:10.130 --> 00:31:15.560
And so if I want to
see, basically, Apple.

00:31:15.560 --> 00:31:19.940
So he went to Apple and Apple's
compromise probability is 0.9,

00:31:19.940 --> 00:31:24.340
so we're already really sure
that the terminal at that Apple

00:31:24.340 --> 00:31:25.295
Store--

00:31:25.295 --> 00:31:26.920
someone's fiddled
with that and they're

00:31:26.920 --> 00:31:29.750
stealing people's information.

00:31:29.750 --> 00:31:33.947
Just to investigate further,
I click another account,

00:31:33.947 --> 00:31:35.780
and I can see their
fraud score is at a two.

00:31:35.780 --> 00:31:37.654
So even though Apple's
compromise probability

00:31:37.654 --> 00:31:39.410
is a 0.9, that
doesn't necessarily

00:31:39.410 --> 00:31:42.050
mean that their taking
everyone's information,

00:31:42.050 --> 00:31:45.240
because not everyone is
vulnerable right now.

00:31:45.240 --> 00:31:47.810
So that's just another
way of drawing connections

00:31:47.810 --> 00:31:50.210
between merchants
and customers, even

00:31:50.210 --> 00:31:52.427
to the second degree
of the other customers.

00:31:55.290 --> 00:31:57.620
This is a dump of
all the affected

00:31:57.620 --> 00:32:00.220
transactions that our
systems are picking up.

00:32:00.220 --> 00:32:01.880
Me as a user, I can
just look at that

00:32:01.880 --> 00:32:06.750
and see trends
maybe in category.

00:32:06.750 --> 00:32:10.626
And then this graph right
here-- it's a little bit busy,

00:32:10.626 --> 00:32:12.750
but it's the total turnover,
so the amount of money

00:32:12.750 --> 00:32:15.630
our customers are
spending at merchants

00:32:15.630 --> 00:32:18.110
based on the categories.

00:32:18.110 --> 00:32:20.760
So since we've already been
investigating and looking

00:32:20.760 --> 00:32:25.700
at Apple, I want to
see that day 166 again.

00:32:28.490 --> 00:32:30.670
What's been going on
in the tech category?

00:32:30.670 --> 00:32:33.070
So I think this color
is tech right here.

00:32:33.070 --> 00:32:37.560
That's day 166, so there
was a bit of a peak there.

00:32:37.560 --> 00:32:40.600
And since it was a
$3,000 transaction only,

00:32:40.600 --> 00:32:43.090
usually tech products
are fairly expensive.

00:32:43.090 --> 00:32:45.580
You know, you buy like a phone
or a computer or something

00:32:45.580 --> 00:32:46.360
like that.

00:32:46.360 --> 00:32:48.715
$3,000 is maybe only
a few purchases,

00:32:48.715 --> 00:32:53.260
so that could be one swipe
of a fraudulent swipe made

00:32:53.260 --> 00:32:55.559
by a fraudster.

00:32:55.559 --> 00:32:57.600
So this whole time, we're
just taking information

00:32:57.600 --> 00:32:59.380
in from so many
different sources

00:32:59.380 --> 00:33:01.100
and starting to
build this picture.

00:33:04.420 --> 00:33:07.550
OK, so the whole time in
the background, our systems

00:33:07.550 --> 00:33:10.600
are working to detect the fraud.

00:33:10.600 --> 00:33:12.350
Even though right now
I'm looking at this,

00:33:12.350 --> 00:33:14.414
it's always working
in the cloud.

00:33:14.414 --> 00:33:15.830
And I put this
button in here just

00:33:15.830 --> 00:33:19.066
to sort of indicate when
our system detects a fraud.

00:33:19.066 --> 00:33:21.010
It would pop up
with this banner,

00:33:21.010 --> 00:33:23.570
and it would follow me around
until I click Detected.

00:33:23.570 --> 00:33:26.810
So then that brings me down to
this attention-grabbing little

00:33:26.810 --> 00:33:30.660
box, where me as a user, I
can start to take action.

00:33:30.660 --> 00:33:33.920
I can get all the
crucial information

00:33:33.920 --> 00:33:36.290
that I need in the most
efficient and effective way

00:33:36.290 --> 00:33:40.170
to start to make decisions
and do something about it.

00:33:40.170 --> 00:33:42.575
So I can see this is the
alert, ID tied with that.

00:33:42.575 --> 00:33:47.360
It was a $5,000 transaction.

00:33:47.360 --> 00:33:49.100
I can look further
into the customer.

00:33:49.100 --> 00:33:50.470
So it was John Smith.

00:33:50.470 --> 00:33:53.330
We had applied that
eight fraud score to him,

00:33:53.330 --> 00:33:57.020
and then he ended up having
his information used illegally.

00:33:57.020 --> 00:33:58.760
And the merchant, it was Apple.

00:33:58.760 --> 00:34:02.030
So based on all of that
information that we've

00:34:02.030 --> 00:34:06.410
been looking at, we already sort
of knew that this would happen.

00:34:06.410 --> 00:34:10.480
And I can even click
Apple's address,

00:34:10.480 --> 00:34:14.940
and this brings me to the fraud
transaction by location page.

00:34:14.940 --> 00:34:18.610
This is a-- it's a Google
Maps API, integrated

00:34:18.610 --> 00:34:23.630
with JavaScript, and it
can populate based on fraud

00:34:23.630 --> 00:34:26.190
that we're picking up in
different areas in the world.

00:34:26.190 --> 00:34:28.929
It can start to populate
and create hotspots.

00:34:28.929 --> 00:34:33.110
So you can see the Apple
Store was in New York City.

00:34:33.110 --> 00:34:34.880
So this is New York right here.

00:34:34.880 --> 00:34:36.380
That's already a hotspot.

00:34:36.380 --> 00:34:38.750
That's what our system's
already been picking up.

00:34:38.750 --> 00:34:42.770
Now unfortunately, the data
that we were working with,

00:34:42.770 --> 00:34:47.750
it was simulated from a
bank in one town in Spain.

00:34:47.750 --> 00:34:50.840
So we are super confident
that moving forward,

00:34:50.840 --> 00:34:53.252
we can easily populate
things like that

00:34:53.252 --> 00:34:54.710
and then start to
build connections

00:34:54.710 --> 00:34:57.980
between the actual
geographic location,

00:34:57.980 --> 00:35:02.210
create hotspots, and then
their connections to CPP.

00:35:02.210 --> 00:35:05.060
So this whole time, we've
been looking at fraud

00:35:05.060 --> 00:35:08.450
after the fact-- more of a
reactive approach to things.

00:35:08.450 --> 00:35:09.890
Let's switch things up.

00:35:09.890 --> 00:35:12.980
And we're going to change to
a more proactive approach.

00:35:16.414 --> 00:35:17.830
So this right here,
in my opinion,

00:35:17.830 --> 00:35:21.990
this is one of the most
important, informative metrics

00:35:21.990 --> 00:35:23.393
on the entire demo.

00:35:23.393 --> 00:35:27.300
So it has two different
sources of information.

00:35:27.300 --> 00:35:29.970
The histogram is showing
us the fraud count

00:35:29.970 --> 00:35:32.310
or the number of fraud
transactions associated

00:35:32.310 --> 00:35:34.710
with that merchant.

00:35:34.710 --> 00:35:38.280
And then the line graph is
showing the average amount

00:35:38.280 --> 00:35:40.050
of time after one
of our customers

00:35:40.050 --> 00:35:43.959
visits that merchant, to when
their information is stolen.

00:35:43.959 --> 00:35:45.750
So this gives us a lot
of power, because we

00:35:45.750 --> 00:35:48.360
can start to look into
same day-- industry

00:35:48.360 --> 00:35:50.040
first, same day detection.

00:35:50.040 --> 00:35:54.690
But I know that
these two merchants

00:35:54.690 --> 00:35:57.060
right here, when
our customers visit

00:35:57.060 --> 00:35:59.460
them, within the next
day, their information

00:35:59.460 --> 00:36:02.100
is going to be used illegally.

00:36:02.100 --> 00:36:04.580
And the same goes
for different times.

00:36:04.580 --> 00:36:05.400
This is 12 days.

00:36:05.400 --> 00:36:09.010
I can click that, and I can
already start to take action.

00:36:09.010 --> 00:36:12.360
I'm sitting at my desk at the
office, and I can take action.

00:36:12.360 --> 00:36:16.110
I can give Fred's
Hardware Store a call.

00:36:16.110 --> 00:36:17.610
I can send an
investigator out there

00:36:17.610 --> 00:36:19.840
to actually look
at the terminal.

00:36:19.840 --> 00:36:22.670
Or I can alert every
customer that's been there

00:36:22.670 --> 00:36:26.600
and let them know, hey, keep
an eye out on your account.

00:36:26.600 --> 00:36:27.600
Same goes for customers.

00:36:27.600 --> 00:36:29.830
So we have a just
another dump here

00:36:29.830 --> 00:36:33.790
of likelihood or
probability of fraud

00:36:33.790 --> 00:36:35.650
happening to these accounts.

00:36:35.650 --> 00:36:37.270
And I can click the customer.

00:36:37.270 --> 00:36:39.035
I see it's Sean Price.

00:36:39.035 --> 00:36:41.410
Same thing, give him a call,
send him a text or an email,

00:36:41.410 --> 00:36:44.320
depending on what we
have on their account,

00:36:44.320 --> 00:36:47.860
along with the actual
specific use case of the bank.

00:36:47.860 --> 00:36:49.870
Or we can suspend
the account outright

00:36:49.870 --> 00:36:51.599
because we're so
sure that we're going

00:36:51.599 --> 00:36:52.890
to be losing money soon anyway.

00:36:55.730 --> 00:36:57.920
So once again, we are
super confident with how

00:36:57.920 --> 00:37:00.090
our system has performed.

00:37:00.090 --> 00:37:03.890
That should we get some more
dense information, some more

00:37:03.890 --> 00:37:05.970
things about the fraud
and the transactions,

00:37:05.970 --> 00:37:09.410
we'll be able to extend into
branch ID fraud, and even

00:37:09.410 --> 00:37:10.520
staff ID fraud.

00:37:10.520 --> 00:37:15.080
So that's looking at
more internal threats.

00:37:15.080 --> 00:37:18.280
And then also location, so we
could start to put together

00:37:18.280 --> 00:37:21.970
these hotspots around the
globe, where when we find out

00:37:21.970 --> 00:37:25.960
a customer is entering,
say, a street that is known

00:37:25.960 --> 00:37:28.240
for fraudulent activity,
we can send them a text

00:37:28.240 --> 00:37:30.865
and they look on their phone and
it says, hey, keep an eye out.

00:37:30.865 --> 00:37:33.300
Stuff like that.

00:37:33.300 --> 00:37:36.800
So moving to the system
performance page.

00:37:36.800 --> 00:37:38.870
The last thing here.

00:37:38.870 --> 00:37:42.650
I just wanted to show you guys
the limitations of the data

00:37:42.650 --> 00:37:44.720
that we had.

00:37:44.720 --> 00:37:48.545
So this is the number of
fraudulent transactions

00:37:48.545 --> 00:37:49.850
for every day.

00:37:49.850 --> 00:37:53.780
Out of 180 days,
the simulator only

00:37:53.780 --> 00:37:57.171
injected exactly
40 for every day.

00:37:57.171 --> 00:37:59.420
So obviously that's not
necessarily reflective of what

00:37:59.420 --> 00:38:02.090
would happen in real
life, but we were still

00:38:02.090 --> 00:38:04.900
able to extract such
meaningful information

00:38:04.900 --> 00:38:06.500
and such meaningful
metrics that we're

00:38:06.500 --> 00:38:08.979
really confident that
with some better data,

00:38:08.979 --> 00:38:10.270
the sky's the limit, basically.

